item(750,'P001468','construction','A rational pole ansatz and its Bethe equations',r'The Riccati equation is \(y^{\prime}+y^2+2By+C=0\), with \(B=\sum b_\alpha\Delta(\lambda-a_\alpha)\), \(C=\sum c_\alpha\Delta(\lambda-a_\alpha)\), and \(y=\sum_i\Delta(\lambda-\lambda_i)\). The first root equation has inconsistent indices and omits a \(\Delta\).',r'For \(\Delta(z)=1/z\), distinct fixed points and distinct roots avoiding them, the ansatz solves the equation exactly iff \(\sum_{j\ne i}(\lambda_i-\lambda_j)^{-1}+\sum_\alpha b_\alpha(\lambda_i-a_\alpha)^{-1}=0\) for each i and \(c_\alpha=2b_\alpha\sum_i(\lambda_i-a_\alpha)^{-1}\). Double poles cancel automatically; these conditions cancel all simple poles, and the remaining proper rational function vanishes.',r'\citet[\S3, Theorem~4, pp.~1197--1198, and \S4]{ushveridze1989quasiexact} develops the analytic pole method, referring back to the author\textquotesingle s 1988 FIAN announcements. The residue calculation gives an exact match in the rational specialization, not an unqualified elliptic theorem.', ['ushveridze1989quasiexact'],cls='A',literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',reconstruction='The exact equivalence is deliberately restricted to the rational kernel. Other kernels require additional constant/elliptic compatibility conditions absent from the notebook.')
item(750,'P001468','claim','The addition identity and its corrected special functions',r'For \(x+y+z=0\), \(\Delta(x)\Delta(y)+\Delta(y)\Delta(z)+\Delta(z)\Delta(x)+\Omega(x)+\Omega(y)+\Omega(z)=0\), with odd \(\Delta\) and even \(\Omega\). The trigonometric constant is printed positive and the elliptic expression lacks a half.',r'Correct examples are \((\Delta,\Omega)=(1/z,0)\), \((\omega\cot(\omega z),-\omega^2/3)\), and \((\zeta(z),(\zeta(z)^2-\wp(z))/2)\). In the last case use \((\zeta(x)+\zeta(y)+\zeta(z))^2=\wp(x)+\wp(y)+\wp(z)\) when \(x+y+z=0\).',r'The addition identity is classical; \citet[\S23.10]{nist2026dlmf} gives the Weierstrass identity. \citet[\S3 and \S11]{ushveridze1989quasiexact} places such scalar triangle identities in the quasi-exactly solvable construction. Direct substitution checks both emendations.', ['nist2026dlmf','ushveridze1989quasiexact'],literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',status='the corrected examples satisfy the stated equation; the two literal examples do not.')
item(750,'P001469','theorem','A local Riccati identity from the addition law',r'A solution with a singularity at zero satisfies \(\Delta^{\prime}+\Delta^2=2\Omega+\Omega(0)\).',r'Assume \(\Delta\) is odd meromorphic with \(\Delta(t)=1/t+O(t)\), and \(\Omega\) even and holomorphic at zero. Put \(y=\varepsilon\), \(z=-x-\varepsilon\) in the addition law and let \(\varepsilon\to0\). The finite part gives exactly \(\Delta^{\prime}(x)+\Delta(x)^2=2\Omega(x)+\Omega(0)\). A different pole residue changes the derivative coefficient.',r'The normalization argument for the constant-\(\Omega\) scalar triangle equation is given in \citet[\S3, equation~(27), p.~1197]{ushveridze1989quasiexact}. The limiting expansion supplies the displayed general form.', ['ushveridze1989quasiexact'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',reconstruction='A simple pole with residue one and regularity of \(\Omega\) at zero are indispensable extra hypotheses.')
item(750,'P001469','claim','The claimed general kernel family is underspecified',r'The general solution is written \(\Delta_0(b\lambda)+a\lambda\), with \(\Delta_0\) one of the rational, trigonometric or elliptic examples.',r'A valid normalized family is obtained from a residue-one kernel by the rescaling \(b\Delta_0(b\lambda)\) and a linear addition, together with the correspondingly transformed \(\Omega\). A classification of every solution additionally requires regularity, pole and degeneracy hypotheses. The notebook neither gives these nor states the transformed \(\Omega\).',r'\citet[\S3 and the final elliptic discussion in \S11]{ushveridze1989quasiexact} confirms the functional-equation method, but the retrieved review does not supply an exact theorem with the notebook\textquotesingle s unrestricted quantifiers.', ['ushveridze1989quasiexact'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='partial',confidence='medium',reconstruction='The prefactor b is needed only when maintaining residue one; the original unnormalized family can still satisfy an appropriately rescaled functional equation.',status='the displayed families are meaningful, but the claimed exhaustive classification is not identified precisely enough to adopt.')
item(750,'P001469-P001470','method','Logarithmic derivatives linearize the Riccati equation',r'Putting \(y=f^{\prime}/f\) is said to give a Schrodinger equation; the later page writes \(f^{\prime\prime}+2Bf^{\prime}+Cf=0\).',r'On an interval where \(f\ne0\), the substitution is equivalent to \(f^{\prime\prime}+2Bf^{\prime}+Cf=0\). Conversely, any Riccati solution gives a local nonzero f by exponentiating its integral. A gauge and coordinate change are still needed to obtain Schrodinger normal form; the first substitution alone leaves a first derivative.',r'\citet[\S\S2--3]{ushveridze1989quasiexact} gives the analytic normal-form method. The equivalence follows directly from \(y^{\prime}+y^2=f^{\prime\prime}/f\).', ['ushveridze1989quasiexact'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The local nonvanishing condition and distinction between a linear ODE and Schrodinger normal form are explicit.')
item(750,'P001469','construction','Electrostatic interpretation of the rational roots',r'The fixed charges are \(b_\alpha\) at \(a_\alpha\), moving charges are one at \(\lambda_i\), and the potential is written as a positive logarithm. Trigonometric and theta analogues are mentioned.',r'For real distinct ordered roots and real fixed points, the rational Bethe equations are the stationarity equations of \(U=-\sum_{i<j}\log|\lambda_i-\lambda_j|-\sum_{i,\alpha}b_\alpha\log|\lambda_i-a_\alpha|\). Positive b produces repulsion with this sign. The logarithm of sine gives the trigonometric force; an elliptic force must use a sigma/theta logarithmic derivative with its possible linear term specified.',r'\citet[\S5, equations~(59)--(63), pp.~1210--1212]{ushveridze1989quasiexact} gives the electrostatic interpretation and real-interval stability assumptions. A positive logarithmic potential with minimization reverses the repulsive-energy sign.', ['ushveridze1989quasiexact'],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',development='annotated',reconstruction='The real domain and sign convention are fixed. The elliptic theta kernel is not silently equated with Weierstrass zeta without its linear correction.')
item(750,'P001469','claim','Quantum and classical stability are not an unrestricted equivalence',r'The same \(\lambda_i\) are called wavefunction nodes, charge positions and Bethe rapidities; quantum stability is asserted equivalent to Coulomb stability.',r'The algebraic root equations admit these interpretations in the matched model. Spectral admissibility also depends on the Hilbert-space measure, self-adjoint domain, and endpoint behaviour. It is not equivalent merely to existence of a stationary electrostatic configuration.',r'\citet[\S5, p.~1212, comparing equations~(45)--(46) with (62)--(63)]{ushveridze1989quasiexact} explicitly says the classical and quantum interval conditions almost coincide, with shifted numerical thresholds in that paper\textquotesingle s normalization. Thus the author\textquotesingle s later primary account itself prevents the literal biconditional.', ['ushveridze1989quasiexact'],cls='A',literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',reconstruction='Only the shared algebraic Bethe equations are identified; physical stability notions require separate definitions.',status='the model-dependent correspondence is established; the unqualified stability equivalence is false.')
item(750,'P001469-P001470','method','A common eigenspace yields partial exact solvability',r'For commuting \(H_0,H_1,H_2\), form \(H=H_0+L_1(H_1-c_1)+L_2(H_2-c_2)\). Common eigenvectors with the chosen eigenvalues remain known.',r'On a common invariant domain, let \(\mathcal V=\ker(H_1-c_1)\cap\ker(H_2-c_2)\) and suppose \(H_0\mathcal V\subset\mathcal V\). Then \(H|_{\mathcal V}=H_0|_{\mathcal V}\). In particular every known \(H_0\)-eigenvector in \(\mathcal V\) remains an H-eigenvector; no commutation of the \(L_j\) with \(H_i\) is needed. A finite-dimensional nonzero \(\mathcal V\) provides an algebraically solvable sector, not the full spectrum.',r'\citet[\S9, equation~(113), p.~1232]{ushveridze1989quasiexact} states exactly this operator construction, in a general finite sum. The review appeared in 1989 after the FIAN work cited in the notes.', ['ushveridze1989quasiexact'],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Domain and invariance hypotheses are supplied; self-adjointness of H is an additional analytic requirement, not automatic from the formula.')
item(750,'P001470','remark','The nonlinear-Schrodinger and oscillator analogy',r'Two lines compare quantum nonlinear Schrodinger theory with the harmonic oscillator, and energy with a particle-number operator; spectra are said to come from Bethe equations.',r'The Hamiltonians, representation spaces, boundary conditions and precise correspondence are absent, so no exact spectral equivalence can be stated.',r'\citet[\S\S7--9]{ushveridze1989quasiexact} supplies related integrable-spin and quasi-exact constructions, but does not identify these two bare correspondences as written.', ['ushveridze1989quasiexact'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',confidence='low',status='unidentified beyond the integrable-system analogy.')
item(750,'P001470','construction','The Liouville transform to Schrodinger form',r'Multiply by an arbitrary A, set \(x=\int d\lambda/\sqrt A\), eliminate the first derivative, and obtain a potential depending on A.',r'On a real interval with \(A(\lambda)>0\), set \(dx/d\lambda=A^{-1/2}\), \(P=2B\sqrt A-A^{\prime}/(2\sqrt A)\), and \(\Psi=A^{-1/4}\exp(\int B\,d\lambda)f\). Then \(-\Psi_{xx}+V\Psi=0\), where \(V=\tfrac12 P_x+\tfrac14P^2-AC\). Complex versions need a local branch of \(\sqrt A\).',r'\citet[\S2, transformation formulas leading to equation~(24)]{ushveridze1989quasiexact} develops the coordinate/gauge procedure. The displayed formula is a direct reconstruction from the notebook\textquotesingle s equation (12).', ['ushveridze1989quasiexact'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The gauge factor, positivity/branch condition and derivatives are made explicit; a coordinate change alone does not remove the first derivative.')
item(750,'P001470-P001471','claim','Separating a spectral constant from the potential',r'The product \(A=\prod_\alpha\Xi(\lambda-a_\alpha)\), \(\Xi^{\prime}/\Xi=\Delta\), is said to put all root information into an x-independent energy.',r'For the rational kernel take A proportional to \(\prod_\alpha(\lambda-a_\alpha)\). Then \(AC\) is polynomial of degree at most \(N-2\), because the Bethe equations imply \(\sum c_\alpha=0\). For \(N=2\) it is a constant energy. For \(N>2\), additional root-dependent coefficients generally remain in the potential and must be fixed to obtain several eigenstates of one Hamiltonian.',r'\citet[\S4, equations~(38)--(43), pp.~1199--1201]{ushveridze1989quasiexact} distinguishes the infinitely degenerate spectral parameter from the others and makes the N-dependent distinction. The product choice alone does not prove an all-N single-energy separation.', ['ushveridze1989quasiexact'],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',development='annotated',reconstruction='A nonzero scalar multiple can be chosen for positivity on the desired real interval; the source\textquotesingle s bare product may have negative sign there.')
item(750,'P001471','theorem','Two fixed positive charges and Jacobi polynomials',r'For \(N=2\), \(b_1,b_2>0\), repulsion traps M particles between \(a_1<a_2\), giving a solution with M nodes for every M and an exactly solvable model.',r'Let \(a_1<a_2\) be real, \(b_1,b_2>0\), and \(M\geq0\) an integer. On the chamber \(a_1<\lambda_1<\cdots<\lambda_M<a_2\), the negative-log energy is strictly convex and tends to infinity at its boundary, hence has a unique minimizer. The monic polynomial \(f_M=\prod_i(\lambda-\lambda_i)\) satisfies \(Af_M^{\prime\prime}+2BAf_M^{\prime}+E_Mf_M=0\), \(A=(\lambda-a_1)(a_2-\lambda)\), \(E_M=M(M-1+2b_1+2b_2)\). These are rescaled Jacobi polynomials, orthogonal with weight \((\lambda-a_1)^{2b_1-1}(a_2-\lambda)^{2b_2-1}\).',r'\citet[\S4, N=2 case, and \S5]{ushveridze1989quasiexact} gives the exactly-solvable and electrostatic interpretations. The convexity and coefficient comparison justify this precise reconstruction; physical boundary conditions after the Liouville transform must still be selected.', ['ushveridze1989quasiexact'],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',reconstruction='The chosen chamber, energy sign, self-adjoint weighted realization and positive A fix the implicit analytic setup.')
item(750,'P001471-P001472','claim','Exterior intervals and finitely many admissible levels',r'For M particles to the right of the second fixed charge, the stability condition is written \(b_1+b_2+M<0\); failure for large M yields only finitely many levels.',r'With \(b_2>0\), the displayed inequality is a sufficient confining condition for the negative-log energy on the exterior chamber, but it is not generally the sharp threshold. Already for M=1, \(a_1=0,a_2=1,b_1=-1,b_2=1/2\), \(U=\log\lambda-\tfrac12\log(\lambda-1)\) has a strict minimum at \(\lambda=2\), although \(b_1+b_2+M=1/2\). Finiteness of normalizable quantum states requires its own endpoint and measure analysis.',r'\citet[\S5, equations~(62)--(63) and the following comparison, pp.~1211--1212]{ushveridze1989quasiexact} treats stable intervals and distinguishes quantum conditions. The notebook\textquotesingle s conclusion is qualitative; no exact level count or necessary-and-sufficient bound is inferred.', ['ushveridze1989quasiexact'],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',development='annotated',reconstruction='The counterexample only rejects necessity of the displayed condition; it does not reject that condition as a sufficient bound.',status='finite admissible spectra occur in the relevant models; the bare inequality is not a universal sharp quantum bound.')
corr('P001468','Equation (5), root condition','summation index i also free; b_alpha(lambda_i-a_alpha) lacks Delta',r'for each i: sum_{j ne i} Delta(lambda_i-lambda_j)+sum_alpha b_alpha Delta(lambda_i-a_alpha)=0 in the rational specialization','Scan verified. Cancelling the residue at lambda_i in y prime+y squared+2By+C forces this equation; the author 1989 section3 gives the matching pole method.')
corr('P001468','Example (7)2',r'Omega=omega^2/3',r'Omega=-omega^2/3','Scan verified. For x+y+z=0, cot(omega x)cot(omega y)+cyclic=1; the written plus-Omega convention therefore requires the negative constant.')
corr('P001468','Example (7)3',r'Omega=zeta^2-wp',r'Omega=(zeta^2-wp)/2','Scan verified. The Weierstrass identity (zeta(x)+zeta(y)+zeta(z))^2=wp(x)+wp(y)+wp(z) gives the factor1/2; NIST DLMF23.10 supplies the addition identity.')
corr('P001469','Repulsive electrostatic potential and minimum','q_i q_j log absolute distance, followed by repulsion and a minimum','use minus q_i q_j log absolute distance for the repulsive energy convention','Differentiation shows the Bethe equations are critical points with either overall sign, but the trapping/minimum argument uses negative logarithms.')
corr('P001469','Stability assertion','quantum stability iff Coulomb stability','model-specific algebraic correspondence; check quantum domains and endpoint thresholds separately','Ushveridze 1989 section5 p1212 explicitly distinguishes the classical and quantum stability bounds, saying they almost coincide.')
corr('P001469','Equation (8), pole normalization','a solution with a singularity at zero','assume a simple pole of residue1 and Omega regular at zero','The epsilon-to-zero derivation has the derivative multiplied by the pole residue; unit residue is needed for the stated Riccati identity.')
corr('P001471','Exterior stability bound','b1+b2+M<0 as the stability condition','a sufficient, not universal necessary-and-sufficient, bound; specify the energy and endpoints','For M1,a1=0,a2=1,b1=-1,b2=1/2, U=log(lambda)-0.5log(lambda-1) has strict minimum at2 although the displayed sum is0.5. The condition is stronger than required here.')
save()

item(751,'P001472-P001473','construction','The finite-dimensional Morse complex',r'For a compact manifold and generic metric, trajectories from \(x_1\) to \(x_2\) have dimension \(\operatorname{ind}(x_1)-\operatorname{ind}(x_2)-1\), and signed index-one trajectories define the differential. The vector field is written \(+\operatorname{grad}f\).',r'For a Morse function on a closed manifold and a Morse--Smale metric, use the negative gradient. The unparametrized trajectory space from p to q has dimension \(\operatorname{ind}p-\operatorname{ind}q-1\). Choose orientations of unstable manifolds; signed counts for index difference one define \(d:C_i\to C_{i-1}\), with \(d^2=0\), and its homology is the ordinary homology. Mod-two coefficients avoid the orientation choices.',r'\citet[\S3, pp.~287--288]{atiyah1988invariants} recalls this construction after \citet[\S2]{witten1982supersymmetry}. The notebook combines positive-gradient notation with the negative-gradient index convention.', ['atiyah1988invariants','witten1982supersymmetry'],literal='false as written',development='annotated',reconstruction='Closedness, Morse--Smale transversality, quotient by time translation and coherent orientation choices are stated explicitly.')
item(751,'P001473','construction','The irreducible gauge quotient',r'The main manifold is \(X=C/G\), irreducible Hermitian connections on the trivial rank-two bundle, with \(G=SU(2)^M\).',r'Let M be a closed connected oriented smooth three-manifold. On the trivial \(SU(2)\)-bundle, connections form \(\mathcal A=d+\Omega^1(M;\mathfrak{su}_2)\). An irreducible connection has stabilizer the centre \(\{\pm1\}\); after Sobolev completion, the effective gauge group \(\mathcal G/\{\pm1\}\) acts freely and the quotient has local Coulomb slices. Reducible points are excluded from this manifold assertion.',r'\citet[\S2, pp.~286--287]{atiyah1988invariants} gives precisely the irreducible quotient used in the Casson/Floer picture. The trivial-bundle condition is automatic for \(SU(2)\)-bundles over a three-dimensional CW complex, but is retained from the notes.', ['atiyah1988invariants'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Orientation, completion and the ineffective central stabilizer are restored.')
item(751,'P001473-P001474','construction','The Chern--Simons one-form and its periods',r'\(\nu_A(B)=\int_M\operatorname{tr}(F_A\wedge B)\) descends to the quotient and is closed. A primitive is identified with a transgression integral on \(M\times[0,1]\); Gelfand refers to Gabrielov--Gelfand--Losik.',r'For closed oriented M, gauge invariance and the Bianchi identity make \(\nu\) basic. On \(\mathcal A\), a primitive is \(\tfrac12\int_M\operatorname{tr}(A\wedge dA+\tfrac23A\wedge A\wedge A)\). Without the half, its differential is \(2\nu\). With a conventional integral normalization, large gauge transformations change the primitive by periods, so on the quotient the functional is circle-valued or defined on a covering.',r'\citet[\S3, pp.~287--288]{atiyah1988invariants} describes the closed one-form, the Chern--Simons functional and its integral periods. The historical Gelfand attribution is recorded as an intervention, not treated as an independent new result.', ['atiyah1988invariants'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated',reconstruction='The factor two and integral normalization are distinguished; the notebook suppresses both.')
item(751,'P001474','construction','Flat critical points and the tangent quotient',r'Critical points are flat connections, hence representations of \(\pi_1(M)\). The quotient tangent space is written with differential forms on X instead of M.',r'The zero set of \(\nu\) consists of flat connections. Gauge-equivalence classes correspond to conjugacy classes of \(\rho:\pi_1(M)\to SU(2)\). At an irreducible connection, the tangent quotient is \(\Omega^1(M;\operatorname{ad}P)/d_A\Omega^0(M;\operatorname{ad}P)\), with a Coulomb-slice realization after completion. The base of these differential forms is M, not the infinite-dimensional quotient X.',r'\citet[\S2, pp.~286--287]{atiyah1988invariants} states the flat-connection/representation correspondence; the quotient formula follows by differentiating the gauge action.', ['atiyah1988invariants'],literal='false as written',development='annotated',reconstruction='The notation error X is replaced by M; the tangent quotient is for the irreducible stratum.')
item(751,'P001475','claim','Twisted first cohomology is not ordinary homology',r'The Hessian is \(\int_M\operatorname{tr}(d_Aa\wedge a)\). Nondegeneracy is identified with \(H^1(M;E)=0\) for every local system, and then with \(H_1(M;\mathbb Z)=0\). The Russian says \ru{Это эквивалентно}.',r'At a specified irreducible flat connection with holonomy \(\rho\), the Hessian kernel modulo gauge is \(H^1(M;\operatorname{ad}\rho)\). An integral homology sphere need not have this group zero. Conversely, vanishing of \(H^1(M;E)\) for every finite-dimensional characteristic-zero local system does not detect integral torsion.',r'\citet[p.~287 and p.~289]{atiyah1988invariants} explicitly requires perturbations when flat critical points are degenerate. Section~\ref{dev:twisted-h1} gives independently checked counterexamples: \(\mathbb{RP}^3\) for the reverse implication, and the connected sum of two Poincare spheres with an irreducible \(SU(2)\) representation for the forward implication.', ['atiyah1988invariants','hatcher2002algebraic'],literal='false as written',development='annotated',reconstruction='The local system governing this Hessian is the adjoint system of the chosen representation, not all local systems at once.',status='the pointwise twisted-cohomology criterion is established; both directions of the notebook\textquotesingle s ordinary/twisted equivalence are false.')
item(751,'P001475','construction','Spectral flow and the relative grading modulo eight',r'Index difference is called the number of points where a zero mode appears, then spectral asymmetry; it is said to be well defined modulo eight.',r'For a path of self-adjoint Fredholm gauge-fixed Hessians with nondegenerate ends, spectral flow is the signed crossing count with multiplicity (or its perturbation-invariant definition), not the unsigned number of zero events. In the \(SU(2)\) instanton theory of an integral homology sphere, changing a path by a generating gauge loop changes this index by eight; hence the relative grading descends modulo eight.',r'\citet[\S4, p.~289]{atiyah1988invariants} gives the spectral-flow calculation and its index-theorem origin. \citet{floer1988instanton} establishes the resulting mod-eight graded homology, received 3 May and published June 1988; only its publisher abstract, not its full proof, was read here.', ['atiyah1988invariants','floer1988instanton'],cls='B',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',development='annotated',reconstruction='Signs, multiplicities, gauge fixing and the homology-sphere gauge-theory setup are required for the stated eight-periodicity.')
item(751,'P001476','construction','A cotangent intersection recovers the Euler characteristic',r'Take the zero section and the graph of df; intersections are critical points, with signed total written \(\chi(T^*L)\).',r'For a closed smooth manifold L and Morse f, the zero section and \(\Gamma_{df}\) in \(T^*L\) meet transversely at the critical points. With compatible orientations the local sign is \((-1)^{\operatorname{ind}f}\), and their sum is \(\chi(L)=\chi(T^*L)\), the last equality following from deformation retraction.',r'\citet[\S5]{atiyah1988invariants} uses Lagrangian intersections as the finite-dimensional model. The equality with the total-space Euler characteristic is harmless here, although the total space is noncompact.', ['atiyah1988invariants'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Compactness of L, Morse nondegeneracy and the orientation convention are supplied.')
item(751,'P001476-P001477','construction','The closed action one-form and Maslov relative index',r'On paths from \(L_1\) to \(L_2\), define \(\nu_\gamma(\xi)=\int_0^1\omega(\dot\gamma,\xi)dt\); zeros are intersections, and a relative index is obtained from Maslov theory.',r'Let \((N,\omega)\) be symplectic and \(L_1,L_2\) Lagrangian. The action one-form on a suitable Sobolev path space is closed because its exterior derivative is the endpoint difference of \(\omega\), which vanishes on the Lagrangians. Its zeros are constant paths in \(L_1\cap L_2\). Transverse intersections give nondegenerate Fredholm Hessians; relative grading uses the Maslov index, with ambiguity determined by the relevant loop classes. Exactness or a covering is needed for a single-valued action.',r'\citet[\S5, pp.~292--293]{atiyah1988invariants} describes the symplectic Floer construction and cites Floer\textquotesingle s 1987 preprints. The index formalism has earlier variational background in \citet[\S2]{duistermaat1974morse}.', ['atiyah1988invariants','duistermaat1974morse'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Lagrangian boundary conditions, Sobolev/Fredholm setup and the action-period ambiguity are explicit; existence of a full Floer homology needs further compactness hypotheses.')
item(751,'P001477','construction','Heegaard splitting as a Lagrangian representation-space intersection',r'A three-manifold is cut into two pieces along a surface; fundamental groups and their representations give the finite-dimensional counterpart of the connection construction.',r'For a genus-g Heegaard splitting \(M=W_1\cup_\Sigma W_2\), each handlebody group is free of rank g and van Kampen presents \(\pi_1M\) as their amalgam over \(\pi_1\Sigma\). On the smooth irreducible locus, the \(SU(2)\) character variety of \(\Sigma\) has its cup-product symplectic form. The handlebody representation loci are Lagrangian where smooth, and their intersection represents flat connections on M. Reducibles, singularities and transversality cannot be ignored.',r'\citet[\S5, pp.~290--293]{atiyah1988invariants} is the direct publication match. It discusses the conjectural relation of the two Floer constructions, but the notebook does not explicitly formulate that homology-isomorphism conjecture, so none is added to this year\textquotesingle s problem index.', ['atiyah1988invariants'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The character-variety smooth locus and Lagrangian hypotheses are supplied rather than claiming that every full representation quotient is a smooth symplectic manifold.')
item(751,'P001477','method','Instantons and cutting a four-manifold along a three-manifold',r'Four-dimensional Yang--Mills equations and cutting a four-manifold into two three-dimensional ones are invoked as the connection to Donaldson theory.',r'On \(M\times\mathbb R\), an anti-self-dual connection in temporal gauge satisfies \(\dot A=-*_MF_A\) for the product orientation, a Chern--Simons gradient equation after choosing the overall sign convention. A decomposition \(Z=Z_1\cup_MZ_2\) has two four-dimensional pieces sharing a three-dimensional boundary. Finite-energy instanton moduli spaces and their boundary data are the objects used in Floer/Donaldson gluing; arbitrary Yang--Mills critical points do not suffice.',r'\citet[\S4, equation~(4.1), and \S7]{atiyah1988invariants} explains the cylinder equation and the gluing programme. \citet{floer1988instanton} is the subsequent rigorous homology construction. The notebook\textquotesingle s dimensions are a literal slip, and its analogy alone is not a complete gluing theorem.', ['atiyah1988invariants','floer1988instanton'],cls='B',literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',reconstruction='Anti-self-duality, finite energy, temporal gauge and the dimensions of the pieces are corrected explicitly.')
corr('P001472','Gradient convention',r'tau=grad f with dim T(x1,x2)=ind(x1)-ind(x2)-1',r'use tau=-grad f for the displayed source-to-target index difference','For the positive gradient f increases along trajectories and the stable/unstable dimension formula reverses. The chain differential shown lowers Morse index.')
corr('P001474','Primitive of nu','integral over M times I of tr(F squared) as the unnormalized primitive','insert the factor1/2 relative to nu(B)=integral tr(F wedge B), then choose integral normalization','Differentiating integral tr(A wedge dA+2/3 A cubed) gives2 integral tr(F wedge B). Overall characteristic-class normalization is also suppressed in the notebook.')
corr('P001474-P001475','Forms and ordinary homology base',r'Omega^i(X) and H_1(X,Z)',r'Omega^i(M) and H_1(M,Z)','X denotes the infinite-dimensional gauge quotient; the covariant differential and the homology-sphere condition are on the original three-manifold M.')
corr('P001475','Equivalence (16)-(17)','H1 with every local system vanishes iff M is a homology sphere','Hessian nondegeneracy at rho iff H1(M;ad rho)=0; ordinary homology is not equivalent','Scan verified by root. RP3 has vanishing twisted H1 over characteristic0 for all finite-dimensional local systems but H1Z=Z/2. The connected sum of two Poincare spheres has an irreducible SU2 adjoint system with H1 dimension3; see dev:twisted-h1.')
